Why this licence and not a permissive one
-----------------------------------------

`Client.exe` statically links `LinearAntiCheat`, a fork of UltimateAntiCheat, which is AGPL v3.
A binary that links AGPL code has to be distributed under the AGPL, so this is the licence the
whole project is offered under rather than a permissive licence that would misdescribe what
ships. The AGPL's network clause is the part that matters in practice: if you run a modified
version of `WebApp.exe` as a public website, people using it are entitled to the source of
your version.

If the client is ever built without the anti-cheat (`-DLINEAR_BUILD_ANTICHEAT=OFF`), the rest
of the project is not entangled with AGPL code and a different licence becomes possible. That
is a decision for the maintainer, not something a build switch changes on its own.

Third-party code
----------------

The vendored trees keep their upstream terms, not this one. Each tree carries its own licence
file, and [Credits](README.md#credits) lists what came from where:

- `LinearAntiCheat` — AGPL v3, `LinearAntiCheat/LICENSE`
- `linearbuild` — CMake, BSD 3-clause, `linearbuild/Licenses/`
- `linearwin`, `linearmath`, `linearui`, `lineargizmo`, `lineartextedit`, `linearphysics`,
  `linearstb` — GLFW, GLM, Dear ImGui, ImGuizmo, ImGuiColorTextEdit, ReactPhysics3D and stb
  respectively, each with its licence file in its tree
- `lineargl` — a generated OpenGL loader, with no separate licence file

Redistributing a build means keeping those notices with it, which `scripts/package-build.ps1`
does.
